<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Edinburgh (1560)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1560">1560</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Treaty of Leith</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> of 6 July 1560 was agreed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> after English forces helped the Scottish Protestant lords besiege the French garrison at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leith">Leith</a>. Both French and English troops withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, ending French domination and opening the way for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> Parliament. Mary, Queen of Scots, never ratified it because it recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>'s right to the English throne.</p></div>
